```js
const d1 = 0b1010, d2 = 0b0111, d3 = 0b1100;
const p = d1 ^ d2 ^ d3;

// d2 is lost
const rebuilt = p ^ d1 ^ d3; // d1 and d3 cancel out
console.log(rebuilt === d2); // true
```
One parity chunk gives one equation. Losing two chunks leaves two unknowns, so you can't solve for both; Reed–Solomon codes add `m` independent parity chunks to survive `m` losses.
